WEBVTT

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:05.586
Jev is not deterministic, and option order is not noise.

00:00:05.586 --> 00:00:08.140
Three claims, needing three different experiments.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:13.137
The complaint: Exact same prompts give you different probabilities when you

00:00:13.137 --> 00:00:14.623
run it multiple times.

00:00:14.623 --> 00:00:15.366
It holds.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:23.942
67.2% of 1,300 items sent three times returned a different probability vector.

00:00:23.942 --> 00:00:26.489
3.3% changed their answer.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:31.373
The published 13% flip rate, split, on 100 items.

00:00:31.373 --> 00:00:35.197
12 of 100: answer changed with the option order.

00:00:35.197 --> 00:00:38.595
5 of 100: answer changed with nothing moved.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:40.370
Both are real.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:43.379
They are not the same thing, and nobody had separated them.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:50.608
Every number here is from the full article, with its sources, at ai.thesatyajit.com.
